  {% set holaflyPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airhub</strong> if you know roughly how much data you'll use and want the lowest price per gigabyte, plus SMS and often a local number bundled in. Pick <strong>Holafly</strong> if you'd rather pay a flat daily rate and never think about a data cap at all. They're not really competing for the same trip: Airhub rewards planning ahead, Holafly charges for not having to.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Cheap Blocks vs. Flat Unlimited</h2>
  <p>
    Airhub sells data in multi-gigabyte blocks (3GB, 4GB) rather than single-day or single-GB units, and in cheaper destinations that lands at genuinely low per-GB prices: Thailand at $0.85 for 1GB and Japan at $1.55 for 1GB. Holafly skips the gigabyte math entirely and charges one flat price per day or per plan for unlimited data, so the bill is the same whether you use 200MB or 20GB that day.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other real difference is what's bundled. Airhub plans include SMS and, in many destinations, a local phone number alongside the data, something Holafly doesn't offer on its standard plans. Neither includes voice calls. If receiving a verification text or handing out a temporary number matters more to you than the data pricing model itself, that tips things toward Airhub regardless of how much data you'll use.
  </p>

